WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.120
Why does water cross a bare lipid bilayer about ten billion times faster than

2
00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:13.740
smaller sodium? Sodium is about 1.0 Å in radius; water is about 1.4 Å.

3
00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:17.860
How do scientists prove which protein moves an ion across?

4
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:22.380
Sodium loses because a bilayer has an oily core.

5
00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:26.560
Gases like oxygen cross by simple diffusion.

6
00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:31.680
Leaving water for oil costs ions and sugars too much energy.

7
00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:34.800
So cells build specific proteins.

8
00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:41.360
To measure transport, scientists follow tracers, detectable labelled copies of a

9
00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:43.260
solute, across sealed membranes.

10
00:00:44.433 --> 00:00:50.013
First, researchers isolate vesicles: small, sealed bubbles of membrane enclosing

11
00:00:50.013 --> 00:00:56.633
fluid. They add a tracked solute outside, plus any fuel a pump needs, such as ATP.

12
00:00:56.633 --> 00:01:02.733
When transport runs, solute leaves the surrounding fluid and builds up inside.

13
00:01:03.883 --> 00:01:06.743
Yet a cell vesicle still holds many proteins.

14
00:01:08.103 --> 00:01:11.323
To test one candidate alone, scientists purify it.

15
00:01:12.443 --> 00:01:17.543
They place it into bare liposomes, synthetic lipid bubbles, or an insensitive cell.

16
00:01:18.617 --> 00:01:19.917
Pause and predict.

17
00:01:21.237 --> 00:01:25.157
You purify a candidate protein and place it into bare liposomes.

18
00:01:26.397 --> 00:01:29.777
You add the solute outside, and nothing crosses the membrane.

19
00:01:31.157 --> 00:01:33.097
Does that prove you chose the wrong protein?

20
00:01:38.717 --> 00:01:44.857
Not necessarily. A failure in a bare bilayer may only mean a missing partner

21
00:01:44.857 --> 00:01:51.317
protein. Orientation matters too: a pump placed backward pushes the wrong way.

22
00:01:52.377 --> 00:01:57.317
And a channel or passive carrier moves solute only downhill, so without a

23
00:01:57.317 --> 00:02:00.317
concentration or voltage push, nothing crosses.

24
00:02:01.550 --> 00:02:04.370
Controls also guard against false positives.

25
00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:10.090
Protein-free liposomes measure background leak across bare lipids alone.

26
00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:14.450
Without an intact seal, a pump cannot hold a gradient.

27
00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:20.430
In a cell sheet, tight seals similarly limit what slips between neighbours and leaks

28
00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:21.430
back.

29
00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:27.320
Another check asks whether an enzyme sits inside sealed vesicles rather than stuck

30
00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:32.380
outside. Detergent opens membranes its substrate cannot cross.

31
00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:39.960
Suppose intact vesicles show 10% % of the activity, and detergent raises it to 100%.

32
00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:46.740
Hidden equals total minus intact: 100% minus 10% leaves 90% % inside.

33
00:02:47.883 --> 00:02:50.963
These assays unlocked how cells pay for transport.

34
00:02:52.303 --> 00:02:59.023
In 1961, Wilhelm Hasselbach and Madoka Makinose added ATP to isolated muscle

35
00:02:59.023 --> 00:03:02.663
vesicles. Calcium left the surrounding fluid.

36
00:03:04.083 --> 00:03:10.463
Setsuro Ebashi and Fritz Lipmann independently reported ATP-driven uptake in 1962.

37
00:03:11.963 --> 00:03:15.203
The cost was about two calcium ions per ATP split.

38
00:03:15.203 --> 00:03:16.203
Satsuro Ebashi and Fritz Lippmann

39
00:03:16.317 --> 00:03:23.197
Expressed in frog oocytes, large egg cells, human SGLT1 moves two sodium ions per

40
00:03:23.197 --> 00:03:28.837
sugar. Purified rat channel TRPV1 was placed in liposomes.

41
00:03:30.117 --> 00:03:35.757
After a rapid heat jump, its current rose on a time scale of about 3.5 ms.

42
00:03:37.357 --> 00:03:42.697
Isolating membranes and testing purified proteins turns a crowded cell into clear

43
00:03:42.697 --> 00:03:42.977
proof.
